<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Danka system</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as danka seido, jidan system, terauke system, terauke</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The danka system is the long-term affiliation of Japanese households with Buddhist temples, in use since the Heian period: the households (danka) support a temple, which in return provides for their spiritual needs. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimabara-rebellion">Shimabara Rebellion</a> of 1637–38 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> made it compulsory, so that every household had to join the nearest temple and obtain each year its certificate (terauke) that it was not Christian. The certificate was needed to marry, travel or take certain jobs, and those without one were recorded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinin">hinin</a> (non-persons) or executed as Christians. The compulsory system was abolished in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji period</a>, but it survives as a voluntary association.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/danka-system.jpg" alt="Danka system" width="330" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:宗門人別改帳_Shumon_Jinbetsu_Aratamecho.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
